  "text": "In a curved space where distances are described by certain functions, those functions were earlier linked to the potentials that generate the gravitational field. It was later shown that if a particular, somewhat artificial limitation in that curved‑space framework is lifted, the distance expression also contains terms that correspond to the four potentials of the electromagnetic field. The present work argues that even this extended framework still carries an unnecessary constraint, and it proposes an even broader formulation. Moving beyond ordinary flat geometry introduces gravity; moving beyond the first curved‑space model brings in electromagnetism; a further step may account for the mysterious forces that hold an electron together, forces that are not described by Maxwell’s equations. While the paper does not claim to solve the electron’s inner structure, it aims to unify the established laws, showing that removing the lingering restriction simplifies the whole picture and offers new insight into the origin of the basic physical laws."
